# Indexed-loop correctness — reads, in-place writes, nested loops over two
# arrays, and access under an `if`.
#
# HISTORY: this fixture is named for what it proves, bounds-check ELISION —
# `for i := 0; i < len(A); i += 1:` lets the compiler prove A[i] in-range and
# skip the per-element check, emitting `(h_a).data[h_i]` instead of
# `tycho_arr_int_get(h_a, h_i)`.
#
# It stopped proving that for one day. Until 2026-07-29 the elision keyed off
# S_FORRANGE's r_start/r_stop/r_step and the loops here were spelled `for i in
# range(len(A)):`; the loops-cleanup plan rewrote them to
# the three-clause form, which is S_FOR3 and had no elision arm, so all four loops
# silently fell back to the checked accessor. The golden did not move — the fallback is
# the CHECKED path, so the tree got slower and safer, never wrong — which is exactly
# why `make test` could not see it. 223 sequential sites across 97 files lost it.
#
# Phase 27 restored it for S_FOR3 (for3_elidable_arr, src/tychoc.c:8314-8368):
# all four elidable loops below emit `.data[` again. `TYCHOC_NO_BOUNDS_ELISION=1`
# still turns it off. tests/bounds_noelide.ty locks the other side — the
# spellings that must KEEP the check — and bench/guard.sh now asserts the
# emitted C directly, because at -O3 gcc folds the check away by itself and no
# wall-time gate can separate the two forms (numbers in bench/guard.sh:41-62).
fn main():
    a := []int
    for i := 0; i < 20; i += 1:
        push(a, i * 3)

    # read elision
    s := 0
    for i := 0; i < len(a); i += 1:
        s = s + a[i]

    # in-place write elision (a[i] = ...), then read under an `if`
    for i := 0; i < len(a); i += 1:
        a[i] = a[i] + 1
    t := 0
    for i := 0; i < len(a); i += 1:
        if a[i] > 30:
            t = t + a[i]

    # nested loops over two distinct arrays — inner indexes b, outer indexes a;
    # neither array is reassigned or passed to a call, so both elide
    b := []int
    for i := 0; i < 5; i += 1:
        push(b, i + 100)
    cross := 0
    for i := 0; i < len(a); i += 1:
        for j := 0; j < len(b); j += 1:
            cross = cross + a[i] * b[j]

    println("s=" + str(s))
    println("t=" + str(t))
    println("cross=" + str(cross))
